parts:
- text: "I have read the \"Circle Packing in Unit Square\" research task and understand the requirements: pack 26 non-overlapping circles within a unit square to maximize the sum of their radii. The System's baseline score is 1.4750086.\n\nFor my first submission, I will implement a greedy iterative radius expansion algorithm. I'll initialize the 26 circle centers using a jittered grid for better distribution, and assign very small initial radii. Then, in a series of iterations, I will try to expand each circle's radius as much as possible without overlapping other circles or exceeding the unit square boundaries. The order of circles for expansion will be randomized in each iteration to help avoid local optima.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Greedy Radius Expansion with Jittered Grid Init\"\ntags: \"baseline, greedy, radius expansion, initial placement\"\nabstract: \"This submission implements an iterative greedy algorithm to maximize the sum of circle radii. Initial centers\
    \ are placed using a jittered grid, and radii are expanded sequentially in randomized order, constrained by boundaries and other circles, for 500 iterations. This serves as a foundational approach to establish a personal baseline.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import random\n\n  # Set seeds for reproducibility of initial conditions and shuffle order\n  np.random.seed(42) \n  random.seed(42)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square.\n      \n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      \n      # 1. Initial placement of centers: Jittered grid for better initial distribution\n      num_x_grid = 5\n      num_y_grid = 6\n      x_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, num_x_grid)\n      y_coords = np.linspace(0.1,\
    \ 0.9, num_y_grid)\n      \n      grid_centers = []\n      for x in x_coords:\n          for y in y_coords:\n              grid_centers.append([x, y])\n              \n      # Randomly select 26 centers from the grid\n      initial_centers_list = random.sample(grid_centers, n)\n      centers = np.array(initial_centers_list)\n      \n      # Add a small random jitter to prevent perfect symmetry and potential stuck points\n      jitter_strength = 0.02 \n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * jitter_strength * 2 \n      \n      # Ensure centers are within reasonable bounds after jittering, to allow for some radius\n      centers = np.clip(centers, 0.05, 0.95) \n      \n      # 2. Initial small radii\n      radii = np.full(n, 0.001) # Start with a very small radius\n\n      # 3. Iterative Radius Expansion (Greedy)\n      num_iterations = 500 \n      \n      best_sum_radii = 0.0\n      best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      best_radii = np.copy(radii)\n\n      circle_indices = list(range(n))\n\
    \n      for iter_step in range(num_iterations):\n          random.shuffle(circle_indices) # Shuffle order for each iteration\n\n          prev_sum_radii_in_iter = np.sum(radii)\n\n          for i in circle_indices:\n              max_r_i = float('inf')\n\n              # Boundary constraints\n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, centers[i, 0])          # left\n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, 1 - centers[i, 0])       # right\n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, centers[i, 1])          # bottom\n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, 1 - centers[i, 1])       # top\n\n              # Overlap constraints with other circles j\n              for j in circle_indices:\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  \n                  dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  \n                  # new_r_i + radii[j] <= dist_ij => new_r_i <= dist_ij - radii[j]\n                  if dist_ij > 1e-9: # Ensure distance is positive before\
    \ considering as a valid constraint\n                      r_can_be_max_by_j = dist_ij - radii[j]\n                      max_r_i = min(max_r_i, r_can_be_max_by_j)\n              \n              # Ensure radius is non-negative and has a minimum positive value\n              radii[i] = max(1e-9, max_r_i) # Smallest allowed radius\n          \n          current_sum_radii = np.sum(radii)\n\n          # Update best configuration\n          if current_sum_radii > best_sum_radii:\n              best_sum_radii = current_sum_radii\n              best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n              best_radii = np.copy(radii)\n          \n          # Early stopping condition\n          # If the sum of radii did not change significantly within this iteration pass\n          if np.isclose(current_sum_radii, prev_sum_radii_in_iter, atol=1e-8):\n              # For this initial submission, we will let it run all iterations,\n              # but this could be a point for early exit.\n              pass\n\
    \n      # Final check and clip radii to be positive, just in case\n      best_radii[best_radii < 1e-9] = 1e-9 \n      \n      return best_centers, best_radii\n```"
