parts:
- text: "My previous two attempts to improve my score by introducing center adjustments or a different initial placement strategy did not succeed. My best score remains 1.9925759 from my first submission (ID 1275), which used a simple greedy radius expansion from a jittered grid.\n\nI will revert to the core logic of my most successful submission. To potentially improve upon it, I will keep the same initial grid parameters (5x6 grid, 0.02 jitter, 0.05-0.95 clip for centers), but significantly increase the number of iterations for the greedy radius expansion from 500 to 3000. I will also make the early stopping condition slightly stricter (`atol=1e-9`) to ensure convergence while still allowing for thorough exploration within the fixed-center greedy framework.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Greedy Radius Expansion with Original Jittered Grid and More Iterations\"\ntags: \"greedy, radius expansion, initial placement, iteration count, baseline refinement\"\nabstract: \"This\
    \ submission reverts to the initial successful strategy of greedy radius expansion, using the original 5x6 jittered grid for initial center placement and a jitter strength of 0.02. The number of radius expansion iterations has been significantly increased to 3000, with a stricter early stopping condition, to allow for more thorough convergence and potential discovery of better local optima within the fixed-center greedy framework.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import random\n\n  # Set seeds for reproducibility of initial conditions and shuffle order\n  np.random.seed(46) # Changed seed for new experiment\n  random.seed(46) # Changed seed for new experiment\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square.\n      \n      Returns:\n          tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n              - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n              - radii: np.ndarray of shape (26,) with\
    \ radius of each circle\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      \n      # 1. Initial placement of centers: Jittered grid (reverting to parameters of 1275)\n      num_x_grid = 5\n      num_y_grid = 6\n      x_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, num_x_grid)\n      y_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, num_y_grid)\n      \n      grid_centers = []\n      for x in x_coords:\n          for y in y_coords:\n              grid_centers.append([x, y])\n              \n      # Randomly select 26 centers from the grid\n      initial_centers_list = random.sample(grid_centers, n)\n      centers = np.array(initial_centers_list)\n      \n      # Add a small random jitter\n      jitter_strength = 0.02 # Reverted to 0.02\n      centers += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * jitter_strength * 2 \n      \n      # Ensure centers are within reasonable bounds after jittering, to allow for some radius\n      centers = np.clip(centers, 0.05, 0.95) # Reverted to 0.05\n      \n      # 2. Initial small radii\n      radii = np.full(n,\
    \ 0.001) \n\n      # 3. Iterative Radius Expansion (Greedy)\n      num_iterations = 3000 # Significantly increased from 500 \n      \n      best_sum_radii = 0.0\n      best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      best_radii = np.copy(radii)\n\n      circle_indices = list(range(n))\n\n      for iter_step in range(num_iterations):\n          random.shuffle(circle_indices) \n\n          prev_sum_radii_in_iter = np.sum(radii)\n\n          for i in circle_indices:\n              max_r_i = float('inf')\n\n              # Boundary constraints\n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, centers[i, 0])          \n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, 1 - centers[i, 0])       \n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, centers[i, 1])          \n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, 1 - centers[i, 1])       \n\n              # Overlap constraints\n              for j in circle_indices:\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  \n                  dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(centers[i]\
    \ - centers[j])\n                  \n                  if dist_ij > 1e-9: \n                      r_can_be_max_by_j = dist_ij - radii[j]\n                      max_r_i = min(max_r_i, r_can_be_max_by_j)\n              \n              radii[i] = max(1e-9, max_r_i)\n          \n          current_sum_radii = np.sum(radii)\n\n          # Update best configuration\n          if current_sum_radii > best_sum_radii:\n              best_sum_radii = current_sum_radii\n              best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n              best_radii = np.copy(radii)\n          \n          # Early stopping condition\n          if np.isclose(current_sum_radii, prev_sum_radii_in_iter, atol=1e-9): # Reduced atol for stricter convergence\n              break # Stop if no significant improvement\n\n      # Final check and clip radii to be positive, just in case\n      best_radii[best_radii < 1e-9] = 1e-9 \n      \n      return best_centers, best_radii\n```"
