tick: 753
role: model
parts:
- text: "My previous attempts to improve my score have not surpassed my initial best of 1.9925759. This suggests that the initial placement of circles is highly influential when using a fixed-center greedy radius expansion.\n\nTo address this, I will implement a meta-strategy: instead of a single run, I will perform multiple independent greedy radius expansions, each starting from a different random initial configuration of centers. I will then return the packing that achieved the highest total sum of radii across all these attempts. This increases the chance of finding a better local optimum.\n\nI will perform 10 such attempts, each using the original successful jittered grid parameters and 500 iterations for the greedy expansion phase, to ensure the overall execution time remains within limits.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Attempt Greedy Radius Expansion with Jittered Grid\"\ntags: \"greedy, radius expansion, initial placement, multi-start, ensemble\"\nabstract:\
    \ \"This submission employs a multi-start strategy to improve circle packing. It runs the greedy radius expansion algorithm 10 times, each with a freshly generated jittered grid for initial circle center placement. The set of centers and radii yielding the highest total sum of radii across all attempts is then returned, aiming to overcome local optima by exploring diverse initial configurations.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import random\n\n  def greedy_radius_expansion_single_run(centers_init: np.ndarray, radii_init: np.ndarray, \n                                        num_iterations: int, random_seed: int) -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Performs greedy radius expansion given initial centers and radii.\n      \"\"\"\n      np.random.seed(random_seed) # Seed for random shuffles within this run\n      random.seed(random_seed)\n\n      n = centers_init.shape[0]\n      centers = np.copy(centers_init)\n      radii = np.copy(radii_init)\n\n      best_sum_radii = 0.0\n      best_centers\
    \ = np.copy(centers)\n      best_radii = np.copy(radii)\n\n      circle_indices = list(range(n))\n\n      for iter_step in range(num_iterations):\n          random.shuffle(circle_indices) \n          prev_sum_radii_in_iter = np.sum(radii)\n\n          for i in circle_indices:\n              max_r_i = float('inf')\n\n              # Boundary constraints\n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, centers[i, 0])          \n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, 1 - centers[i, 0])       \n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, centers[i, 1])          \n              max_r_i = min(max_r_i, 1 - centers[i, 1])       \n\n              # Overlap constraints\n              for j in circle_indices:\n                  if i == j:\n                      continue\n                  \n                  dist_ij = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  \n                  if dist_ij > 1e-9: \n                      r_can_be_max_by_j = dist_ij - radii[j]\n                      max_r_i = min(max_r_i,\
    \ r_can_be_max_by_j)\n              \n              radii[i] = max(1e-9, max_r_i) # Ensure radius is non-negative and has a minimum positive value\n          \n          current_sum_radii = np.sum(radii)\n\n          if current_sum_radii > best_sum_radii:\n              best_sum_radii = current_sum_radii\n              best_centers = np.copy(centers)\n              best_radii = np.copy(radii)\n          \n          # Early stopping condition\n          if np.isclose(current_sum_radii, prev_sum_radii_in_iter, atol=1e-9):\n              break \n    \n      best_radii[best_radii < 1e-9] = 1e-9 # Final check to ensure positivity\n      return best_centers, best_radii, best_sum_radii\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Construct a packing of 26 circles in the unit square using a multi-start greedy approach.\n    \n    Returns:\n        tuple: A tuple of (centers, radii) where:\n            - centers: np.ndarray of shape (26, 2) with (x, y) coordinates\n            - radii:\
    \ np.ndarray of shape (26,) with radius of each circle\n    \"\"\"\n    n = 26\n    \n    # Parameters for initial grid (from best-performing submission 1275)\n    num_x_grid = 5\n    num_y_grid = 6\n    jitter_strength = 0.02\n    clip_min = 0.05\n    clip_max = 0.95\n    initial_small_radius = 0.001\n    num_greedy_iterations_per_run = 500 \n\n    num_initial_attempts = 10 # Number of different initial random setups to try\n\n    overall_best_sum_radii = 0.0\n    overall_best_centers = None\n    overall_best_radii = None\n\n    for attempt_idx in range(num_initial_attempts):\n        # Generate a distinct seed for each attempt's initial placement and greedy shuffle\n        # A fixed base seed for reproducibility of the overall submission\n        current_base_seed = 42 + attempt_idx \n\n        np.random.seed(current_base_seed) \n        random.seed(current_base_seed)\n\n        # 1. Initial placement of centers: Jittered grid\n        x_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, num_x_grid)\n\
    \        y_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, num_y_grid)\n        \n        grid_centers = []\n        for x_coord in x_coords:\n            for y_coord in y_coords:\n                grid_centers.append([x_coord, y_coord])\n                \n        # Randomly select 26 centers from the grid\n        initial_centers_list = random.sample(grid_centers, n)\n        centers_for_this_attempt = np.array(initial_centers_list)\n        \n        # Add a small random jitter\n        centers_for_this_attempt += (np.random.rand(n, 2) - 0.5) * jitter_strength * 2 \n        centers_for_this_attempt = np.clip(centers_for_this_attempt, clip_min, clip_max) \n        \n        # Initial small radii for this attempt\n        radii_for_this_attempt = np.full(n, initial_small_radius) \n\n        # Run greedy expansion for this initial setup\n        current_best_centers, current_best_radii, current_best_sum_radii = \\\n            greedy_radius_expansion_single_run(centers_for_this_attempt, radii_for_this_attempt,\
    \ \n                                            num_greedy_iterations_per_run, current_base_seed) \n\n        if current_best_sum_radii > overall_best_sum_radii:\n            overall_best_sum_radii = current_best_sum_radii\n            overall_best_centers = np.copy(current_best_centers)\n            overall_best_radii = np.copy(current_best_radii)\n            \n    # Fallback if no valid packing found (should not happen with minimum radius)\n    if overall_best_centers is None: \n        overall_best_centers = np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * n)\n        overall_best_radii = np.full(n, 1e-9)\n\n    return overall_best_centers, overall_best_radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**Rethinking Initial Placement and Iteration Strategies**\n\nMy latest submission didn't improve upon my personal best, indicating that a simple increase in iterations with the same parameters isn't sufficient. The greedy radius expansion method, while effective, is highly sensitive to the initial placement of circle centers.  This suggests that finding a robust way to generate good starting configurations is crucial.  \n\nMy past attempts at better initial placements haven't yielded good results. Random sampling from a jittered grid is hit-or-miss, and the \"Repulsive Initial Placement\" and \"Iterative Relaxation\" ideas performed poorly.  \n\nMy new approach will be to focus on initial placements. Instead of just picking a single initial configuration, I will run the greedy expansion *multiple times*, each time starting with a *different* random initial placement. I will then pick the best result. This is essentially a form of Monte Carlo search for good initial conditions.\n\
  \nSpecifically, I'll use the parameters from my best submission (ID 1275) for the greedy expansion:\n- `num_x_grid = 5`\n- `num_y_grid = 6`\n- `jitter_strength = 0.02`\n- `centers = np.clip(centers, 0.05, 0.95)`\n- `num_iterations = 500` for radius expansion.\n\nI will try this greedy algorithm for multiple initial configurations by setting `num_initial_attempts = 10` runs.  For each attempt, the script generates a unique random seed to setup the initial configuration, which is the jittered grid layout.  I will also set a seed for the greedy expansion step itself, and this is set to be the same value as the initial random configuration generator. This is to ensure reproducible results for each attempt if the same source code is used.\n\nI am setting `num_greedy_iterations = 500` for each attempt, hoping the overall time for this total of 5000 iterations to run will stay within the 20 minute limit.\nThe script will now run this sub-function multiple times and then return the best result\
  \ found (best `(centers, radii)` that yielded the maximum `sum_radii`).  I'm hopeful that this approach will allow me to overcome the limitations of the greedy algorithm's dependence on initial conditions and achieve a better score than my personal best.\n"
